# Narrow false-positive allowlist for scripts/secret-scan.sh (one ERE per line).
#
# Fake values in internal/cli/doctor_shell_alias_test.go: the test asserts that
# an alias VALUE never reaches a doctor Detail, so the fixture has to look like
# a secret. They are not credentials. The key name is spelled with a bracket
# expression so this line does not match the scanner's own pattern.
api_ke[y]=SECRET(123|456)
# The first spelling of the rule above is still in this branch's history
# (commit 5a91cff) and the range scan reads history, so allow that one line.
SECRET[(]123[|]456[)]
# Hygiene sentences in this repository's pipeline reports say that fixtures
# contain no string "shaped" like a secret assignment, and name the key
# patterns in backticks to do so (first seen in docs/reports/{test,verify}-
# 2026-09-19-codex-agmsg-writable-root.md, and in an earlier spelling of
# this comment, commit 90dc4be -- the range scan reads history). The rule
# matches only that idiom: one or more backticked key names that end at the
# "=" with nothing after it, joined by "/", followed by "-shaped". A
# backticked key name alone is NOT enough, and a value between "=" and the
# closing backtick never matches. Key names use bracket expressions so this
# file does not trip the scanner itself. Note the scanner's allowlist works
# per LINE: once a rule matches, nothing else on that line is reported, so a
# line that carries this idiom must not carry anything else worth scanning.
`(api_ke[y]|passwor[d])=`(/`[a-z_]+=`)*-shaped
